  "map_id": "map:amundsen-scott-south-pole-station",
  "method": "Convex hull of the 78 standard string positions of geometry:icecube-strings-1-78 (13 corner vertices, each straight edge subdivided into pieces of 10 m or less in the grid plane so that the validator's longitude-latitude area formula, which is only exact for short edges near the pole, measures the true area). It is an outline computed here to show the footprint of the 1 km3 volume, not a published boundary of the instrumented ice. Frame and anchor: the IceCube coordinate system has its y-axis along the Prime Meridian (Grid North, towards Greenwich) and its x-axis 90 degrees clockwise from it (Grid East) (IceCube instrumentation paper, arXiv v3, section 5.3), and its origin is at elevation 883.9 m. The IceCube Collaboration software repository i3astropy gives the nominal location of the centre of the detector as lat -89.9944, lon -62.6081, height 883.9 m, which is taken as the frame origin. Conversion: the origin is placed at that point in a South Pole azimuthal-equidistant plane (distance from the pole = colatitude x 6 371 008.8 m, grid north = 0 deg meridian, grid east = 90 deg E; this is the same plane the browser draws), each string is the origin plus its (x, y) offset, and the inverse projection gives lat/lon. The origin is 622.7 m from the pole on the 62.6 deg W meridian. Accuracy: the layout of the strings relative to each other is surveyed to about 1 m, and the pattern keeps its true size and orientation (grid north). The absolute position of the whole pattern is only good to about 500 m: the anchor is a nominal value of no stated epoch, the array moves with the ice about 10 m a year relative to geographic coordinates, and the lat/lon of IceCube on Wikipedia and in OpenStreetMap (89.99 S, 63.4531 W) lies 489 m from the anchor used.",
  "name": "IceCube in-ice array footprint (convex hull of strings 1-78)",
  "notes": [
    "Planar area of the hull in the grid plane 867599 m2 (0.868 km2); the official figure is the nominal 1 km3 volume."
  ],
